1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
A spatial question shows one figure and its image, and asks which operation produced the second.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
The syllabus names four operations on shapes: translation, rotation, scaling and mirroring.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
Two more families follow from them: paper folding and cutting, and patterns in two and three dimensions.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
Every question here is answerable by a written test, not by guessing at a picture.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
A translation slides the figure. Nothing about its shape, size or orientation changes.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
A rotation turns it about a point. Size and shape survive; the figure now faces a different way.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
A scaling multiplies every distance by the same factor. Angles survive, lengths do not.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
Only a mirror reverses the figure, and that reversal cannot be undone by turning it in the plane.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
Read three labelled corners clockwise. A rotation keeps that order; a mirror reverses it.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
The L tetromino cannot be turned into its mirror image in the plane at all.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
Eight operations keep a square in place: four rotations and four mirrors.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
Order matters: rotating then mirroring is not the same as mirroring then rotating.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
A line of symmetry folds the figure onto itself. A rotational order counts turns that do the same.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
The square has four lines and rotational order four. The rectangle has two lines and order two.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
The T tetromino has one line; the L tetromino has none, because it is chiral.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
The check: lines plus rotational order equals the size of the symmetry group, and that size divides eight.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
Each fold halves one dimension and doubles the number of layers.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
A 20 by 8 sheet folded three times perpendicular to its long edge gives 10 by 8, then 5 by 8, then 5 by 4.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
The perimeter of the folded sheet is 18 centimetres, and the wrong answer is 21.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
The check on any fold sequence: original area equals final area times the layer count.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
One punch goes through every layer, so holes equal punches times layers.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
A 4 by 4 sheet folded in half twice has four layers, so one punch makes four holes.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
Unfold in the reverse order, reflecting the holes across each fold line as you go.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
The four holes are symmetric about both fold lines, which is why the pattern looks regular.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
A net is a flat arrangement of six squares that folds into a cube. There are exactly eleven.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
Thirty-five free hexominoes exist, and the model rolls a cube across each one: eleven close up.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
Two faces are opposite exactly when they are two steps apart in a straight line.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
Opposite faces can never be adjacent, so they can never both be visible in one view.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
One straight cut through a block adds one piece. Three cuts, one along each axis, give eight.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
With c-x cuts across one axis, the pieces number the product of the three counts plus one.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
Eight equal pieces therefore need exactly three cuts, not four.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
The trap is counting cuts as pieces, or pieces as cuts.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
A tiling question is an arithmetic question. The first test is area.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
The second test is colour: shade the board like a chessboard and count each colour.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
A domino covers one black and one white square, so unequal counts mean no tiling exists.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
Remove two opposite corners of an 8 by 8 board and the counts are 30 and 32: impossible.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:36,000
Step one: name the operation from the wording of the question.

38
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:42,000
Step two: test orientation with three labelled corners before comparing anything else.

39
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:48,000
Step three: for a folding question, halve one dimension, double the layers, and find the long edge again.

40
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:54,000
Step four: for cuts, multiply layers by punches, or use the product formula in three dimensions.

41
00:04:54,000 --> 00:05:00,000
Step five: for tiling, check area and then colour. Two numbers settle it.

